{
  "families": [
    {
      "family": "pipelined",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 14, "high": 18, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 12, "high": 18, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -2, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.55,
      "why": "Full pipelining gives 1 result/cycle, so throughput = Fmax. With N=12..18 stages the critical path is one micro-rotation (short), so Fmax should comfortably exceed 32 MHz, meeting throughput_msps>=32. Accuracy is set by n_iter/data_width; N>=12 with W>=14 and rounding should reach 2^-12. Area is the objective, so I want the smallest W and N that still pass accuracy and the 0.5us batch bound. The 0.5us p99_batch bound is the tight system constraint: a burst of 32 must finish within 0.5us, i.e. the unit must sustain >=64 results/us effective, which full pipelining at Fmax>=64MHz achieves. Searching W 14..18 and N 12..18 brackets the accuracy/area knee; angle_guard and frac_guard are kept low to minimise area while rounding is tested to recover accuracy bits cheaply."
    },
    {
      "family": "pipelined_m",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 14, "high": 18, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 12, "high": 18, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -2, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]},
        {"param": "m", "low": 2, "high": 4, "choices": null}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.45,
      "why": "pipelined_m registers every m stages, cutting flip-flop count and area versus full pipelining while still delivering 1 result/cycle (throughput = Fmax). Fewer registers lengthen the combinational path (m micro-rotations in series), which lowers Fmax; m=2..4 keeps the path short enough that Fmax should still clear the 32 MHz throughput floor and the 64 MHz needed for the 0.5us burst. This family is the most promising for the min-area objective because it trades a little Fmax margin for a real area saving. I search the same accuracy-relevant ranges as pipelined (W 14..18, N 12..18, low guards, both roundings) and add m=2..4 to find the area/Fmax sweet spot that still satisfies the batch-latency bound."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec is area-minimisation subject to three hard constraints: throughput_msps>=32, max_abs_err<=2^-12, and sys_p99_batch_us<=0.5 for a 32-request burst every 1us. The burst bound is the binding system constraint: 32 results in 0.5us requires an effective >=64 results/us, which only the 1-result/cycle families (pipelined, pipelined_m) can meet, and only if Fmax is high enough. Both of my assigned families are 1-result/cycle, so they are the right choice; iterative/unrolled_k are not available to me and would also struggle with the burst. Accuracy depends only on data_width/n_iter/guards/rounding (identical bits across families), so I bracket the accuracy/area knee at W=14..18, N=12..18 with low guards and both roundings to find the smallest design that still clears 2^-12. I split budget 55/45 between pipelined (baseline, highest Fmax margin, safest for the batch bound) and pipelined_m (area-saving via fewer registers, the likely winner for the min-area objective). Keeping ranges narrow focuses the 100-eval budget on the feasible region rather than wasting it on obviously-too-small (fails accuracy) or obviously-too-large (wastes area) corners."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "rationale": "The exploration has identified a feasible Pareto front dominated by the `pipelined` family. The best design (pipelined, data_width=18, n_iter=16, angle_guard=0, frac_guard=0, rounding=round) achieves an area of 44427 um^2, 13.02 bits of accuracy (max_abs_err ~ 1.2e-4, well below the 2.44e-4 limit), and a throughput of 143 MSPS (well above the 32 MSPS requirement). The system constraint `sys_p99_batch_us <= 0.5` is met by the best designs (best seen 0.276 us). The `pipelined_m` family was explored but yielded no feasible designs, likely due to higher latency or area overheads for the same accuracy/throughput trade-off. Since this is the final round and a clear, feasible, and efficient solution exists on the front, stopping is the correct action. The selection rule (min area) will pick the 44427 um^2 design."
,
  "next_plan": null
}
